    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 061930\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1230 PM PDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A band of showers with a chance of embedded thunderstorms \n  continues along the I-15 and I-40 corridors this afternoon.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, with low chances for \n  isolated storm activity later this week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday.\n\nOur band of light to moderate rain has set up along the I-15 and I-\n40 corridors. Rain rates have generally been around 0.10\" per hour \nor less, with rainfall totals across the area ranging from a trace \nto 0.33\" as of 1230PM. Have seen only a few lightning strikes so \nfar, with best odds of additional strikes on the northern edge of \nthe precip band. Barring any more robust convective development, \nanticipating rainfall remain light to moderate. Activity is \nexpected to wane this evening once the shortwave moves through. \nClouds and precip will keep temperatures well below normal for \nearly September. Highs will range from the upper 70s to low 90s \nacross the area, with the values on the higher end likely being \n\"midnight highs\".\n\nAs the Pacific trough lifts northward tomorrow, we lose the forcing \nand some of the moisture that helped produce the widespread rain \ntoday. We'll still have some high clouds around, but drier \nconditions return along with seasonable temperatures. Ensemble \nguidance continues to show a ridge retrograding back into the Desert \nSouthwest later this week, promoting a warming trend through at \nleast Thursday. By that time, the forecast calls for highs 3-7 \ndegrees above normal, with values eclipsing the century mark over \nmost of the Mojave Desert. However, guidance also keeps modest \nmonsoonal moisture in place, allowing low (less than 30%) \nshower/storm chances to linger over the higher terrain. A \nweakening of the ridge and transition to more zonal flow next \nweekend should scour moisture out and bring temperatures down \nslightly.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nrain showers will persist through the afternoon. The timeframe of \nhighest confidence for impacts to the terminal is 18Z to 22Z, during \nwhich reduced visibility down to 5 miles and ceilings as low as 5kft \ncannot be ruled out. Ceilings will improve after sunset as the rain \nband exits the area. Winds will also become less gusty after sunset \nand maintain a southwesterly direction at less than 10 knots. Gusty \nsouthwesterly winds return tomorrow afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...A band of rain showers \nwith embedded thunderstorms will persist through the rest of the \nafternoon along and southeast of Interstate 15. Ceilings to around \n5kft and reductions in visibility are possible at KDAG and at Las \nVegas Valley terminals. There is a 50 to 60 percent probability of \ngusty northerly winds at KEED and KIFP this afternoon. If these \nwinds do not materialize, expect lighter northwesterly winds \ninstead. Lighter winds and clearing skies return this evening as the \nrain band moves out \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
